Complete Diagnosis of All 3 Issues
Issue 1 — The "tool role must follow tool_calls" Error (Stream Error after Triage)
What the error means exactly
"Invalid parameter: messages with role 'tool' must be a response to 
a preceeding message with 'tool_calls'."  param: messages.[1].role
The LLM API is telling you: a tool-result message exists in the conversation history without the corresponding tool-call message that triggered it.
Why it happens — the root cause
The Orchestrator uses add_messages in its state. This means it accumulates every single message from every subgraph into one growing list. After FNOL completes, the state looks like this:
messages = [
  HumanMessage:  "Hi I want to create a claim..."
  AIMessage:     tool_call → get_mandatory_fields      ← from FNOL
  ToolMessage:   result of get_mandatory_fields         ← from FNOL
  AIMessage:     tool_call → get_guidewire_policy       ← from FNOL
  ToolMessage:   result of get_guidewire_policy         ← from FNOL
  AIMessage:     tool_call → create_fnol_submission     ← from FNOL
  ToolMessage:   result of create_fnol_submission       ← from FNOL
  AIMessage:     tool_call → create_claim               ← from FNOL
  ToolMessage:   result of create_claim (CLM-2026-...)  ← from FNOL
  HumanMessage:  "[Orchestrator] FNOL complete. Routing to Triage"
  ... (15–20+ messages deep)
]
Now Triage Agent's chatbot node runs this:
# triaging_agent/graph.py line 238
messages = [SystemMessage(content=SYSTEM_PROMPT)] + state["messages"]
message = await llm_with_tools.ainvoke(messages)
It passes the full accumulated message list to the LLM. The LLM API then validates the sequence and finds:
Message at index [1] has role tool, but the message before it is a SystemMessage — there's no tool_calls message immediately before this tool result.
Alternatively, when the -20 slice is applied in the supervisor, it cuts into the middle of a tool-call/result pair. The tool_call message from FNOL agent rolls off the slice window, but the tool result message for it stays, creating an orphaned tool message.
The exact lines causing this
In orchestrator/graph.py — supervisor uses:
list(messages[-20:])   # slices mid-sequence, may cut off a tool_call
In triaging_agent/graph.py — chatbot uses:
messages = [SystemMessage(content=SYSTEM_PROMPT)] + state["messages"]  # passes ALL messages
The FNOL agent leaves behind many tool-call/tool-result pairs. The Triage Agent's LLM sees these inherited messages and fails validation.
Why it stops specifically at Triage (the 2nd agent)
FNOL is the first agent, so it starts with a clean slate — no inherited tool messages, no error. Triage is the first agent to receive an inherited dirty message history from FNOL. Every agent after FNOL will face this same issue because the message contamination grows with each agent.
Issue 2 — Frontend ↔ Backend Data Disconnection
Architecture of the disconnect
The system has two separate claims data silos in the same PostgreSQL database:
Source	Table Written To	Table Read From (Frontend)
Agents via create_claim	claims (via POST /api/claims)	—
Frontend seeded data	claims_master (via seed scripts)	claims_master (via GET /api/claims-master)
The agents write to claims. The LossAssessment page, ClaimsCockpit, and Dashboard read from claims_master. These are different database tables. There is no automatic sync between them when a new agent-created claim arrives.
This is why:
You run the orchestrator, a claim gets created as CLM-2026-MPXY77TZGXVK in the claims table
You open LossAssessment in the frontend, it calls GET /api/claims-master → your new claim is not there
The frontend only shows the pre-seeded test claims from claims_master
Approval mismatch — the body schema conflict
When the frontend approves a claim (LossAssessment.tsx):
POST /api/claim-decision/approve
{
  claimId: "...",
  claimNumber: "CLM-...",
  coverageStatus: "Confirmed",    // ← REQUIRED by the Express route
  fraudIndicator: "Low",          // ← REQUIRED for validation logic
  complexity: "Medium",           // ← checked: if "High" or "Complex" → blocked
  settlementAmount: 8000,
  stpClassification: "Manual",
  decisionBy: "Adjuster"
}
When the agents approve a claim (decision_router.py):
POST /api/claim-decision/approve
{
  "claimNumber": "CLM-...",
  "approvedBy": "Jarvis Copilot",
  "settlementAmount": 8000,
  "approvalNotes": "..."
}
The Express route at line 3110 does:
if (coverageStatus !== "Confirmed") {
  return res.status(400).json({ error: "Cannot approve: Coverage must be Confirmed" });
}
The agent doesn't send coverageStatus, so coverageStatus is undefined. undefined !== "Confirmed" → the approval silently fails with HTTP 400 even though the agent thinks it succeeded (because http_client.py raises on non-2xx but the agent's catch block swallows it and returns the error string in the result).
Why frontend approvals don't show in the backend agent view
When the frontend approves a claim:
It updates claims_master status and claim_journey_master and creates a record in claim_decisions table
When the agent reads the claim:
It calls GET /api/claims/by-number/{num} which reads from the claims table
These are again different tables. The frontend's approval updates claims_master but the agent reads from claims. The status update done by the frontend does NOT reflect when the agent queries the claim.
Issue 3 — Other Agents (Adjuster, Vendor, Expert, SIU) Status
All agents after Triage will face the same "tool role" error described in Issue 1 because the message contamination grows with each completed agent. They inherit an increasingly long dirty history from all previous agents.
Additionally — the default ports in each agent's server.py:
Agent	Default Port
FNOL Intake	8002
Triaging	8002 ← same as FNOL
Adjuster Copilot	8004
SIU	8007
FNOL and Triage both default to port 8002. If you try to run them as standalone servers simultaneously they will conflict. This only matters for standalone testing — when running via the Orchestrator they are imported as subgraphs (not separate HTTP servers), so there is no port conflict in that scenario.
